1
00:00:04.011 --> 00:00:04.000
By now, you're already very proficient on building some of these use cases, right?

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
You'll learn a lot about how these systems work and what the different components are.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
That said, the gap between building things as a prototype and getting into production is huge.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
There's a lot going on in there.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
So now we're going to start to dive into what actually means to take one of these systems into a production environment.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Let's go into the slides.

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
A lot of these companies and teams that are building these AI agent systems,

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:36.000
they're now getting to a point where they're moving out of a prototyping stage and going into fully-fledged production use cases.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
What can be very hard to do if you're not building this the right way?

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:47.000
One of the biggest jumps is that as a prototype, you're often running these agents as a one-off automation.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.000
So, for example, you saw the crew that we built early that helps you organize and prepare for meetings.

12
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:01.000
We could do another use case where it summarizes transcripts with action items and help you draft emails, for example, as next steps.

13
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
So in the prototype stage, that crew might be triggered manually by you when you're having a meeting.

14
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.000
You run that crew and you get the results.

15
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Or you might have deployed this to run for, say, your engineering team at a small scale.

16
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
But the thing is, once you turn this on to a lab and you turn this to an entire company,

17
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
you're moving away from human time to machine time.

18
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
So in human time, you have fewer users, fewer executions.

19
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
It's very easy to monitor and fix.

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
When we're talking about machine time, we're talking about thousands of users, hundreds of thousands of executions.

21
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.000
What makes it very difficult to monitor and fix at a scale if you didn't put some thought into this early on.

22
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
When you just have a few crew executions in a day, maybe a couple dozen in a day,

23
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.000
it's pretty easy to understand the traces and understand where the problem is and how you fix it.

24
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.000
And you're probably also working with a small set of relatively proficient users.

25
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.000
If it's just you, that's even easier.

26
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:09.000
But as you scale to hundreds, thousands or even hundreds of thousands of executions, you can't monitor those ad hoc anymore.

27
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.000
So you need to make sure that the vulnerabilities don't start to pile up.

28
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.000
So that's what I mean by machine time.

29
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.000
The agents that are getting now into this very fast paced environment, doing many executions,

30
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.000
and you still need to be able to track their performance in some way, shape or form.

31
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.000
So there's two kind of metrics that you really care about in here.

32
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
One is zoom in metrics.

33
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.000
And that means the ability to debug, to reproduce errors, checking traces, prompts, tokens.

34
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:48.000
It's all about zooming in to one specific execution and being able to trace it and understand everything that is going on with that.

35
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.000
The other one is zoom out metrics.

36
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:59.000
And those metrics will be more around using LLM-as-a-judge, checking for quality, hallucinations, ROI or success metrics.

37
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:07.000
The idea here is that you might be able to look at some zoom in metrics for ad hoc debugging.

38
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:16.000
But once that you're running these things at a larger scale, you want to make sure that you have the systems in place to check for quality as a whole for the entire system.

39
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.000
So this is very exciting, but I also want to spend some time talking about the pitfalls.

40
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.000
What are the things that go wrong?

41
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.000
And this is interesting because the tech is there.

42
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.000
You have so many powerful models out there right now.

43
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
And with some of the features that we have shown and the ones that we're going to show ahead, you have so much that you can build already.

44
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:50.000
If anything, what we're finding out is that most of the companies and the teams that are struggling with getting the systems in production, they're having pitfalls on their process, not on their tech.

45
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:03.000
So they're not spending enough time planning these use cases, for example, or they don't have a clear definition of success or not breaking the process into smaller chunks so that you can have many agents use it.

46
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.000
And even if they do have a clear definition of success, then they're not measuring or evaluating it.

47
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.000
And that's an entire new problem.

48
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:21.000
The idea that you deploy these things and you can't tell if they're actually adding value to you or not, it's something that will hinder your ability to make further investments.

49
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.000
And as you improve those systems, you need to be able to tell if you're trending in the right direction.

50
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:34.000
So now that you know some of the challenges of these production systems, it's important that you understand how to actually address those.

51
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.000
So in the next video, you see many different tactics you have with CrewAI to debug, observe and optimize your multi-agent systems.

52
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:49.000
So you want to make sure that you don't miss the next lesson, because that's going to be a cornerstone on some of the new examples further ahead.
